# Vulnerability details

## Impact
An operator can create a vault with a certain asset that exhibits unexpected behavior, which can result in errors for the operator or, worse, malicious use.

## Proof of Concept
When an operator creates a vault, they can do so with any allowlisted vault and any allowlisted asset.

````
    function validateVaultConfigs(Storage storage self, VaultLib.Config[] calldata vaultConfigs, address implementation)
        public
        view
    {
        if (!(implementation == address(0) || isVaultImplAllowlisted(self, implementation))) {
            revert VaultImplNotAllowlisted();
        }
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < vaultConfigs.length; i++) {
            if (self.assetSlashingHandlers[vaultConfigs[i].asset] == address(0)) revert AssetNotAllowlisted();
        }
    }
````
This function in the CoreLib library (L-77) will cause the call to the deployVaults function to revert. It reverts if the vault or the asset is not allowlisted. However, the function does not check the compatibility between the asset and the vault.

This means that an operator can, for instance, deploy a vault with the native vault implementation but with an ERC20 token as the asset. This can lead to unexpected behavior depending on what will be the allowlisted vaults or the allowlisted assets in the future.

You can copy and paste this test in the vault.t.sol :

````
function test_deployVaultsWithNativeAssets() public {
        VaultLib.Config[] memory vaultConfigs = new VaultLib.Config[](1);
        vaultConfigs[0] = VaultLib.Config({
            asset: Constants.DEAD_BEEF,
            decimals: 18,
            operator: operator,
            name: "TestVault",
            symbol: "TV",
            extraData: bytes("")
        });
         address[] memory assets = new address[](1);
        assets[0] = Constants.DEAD_BEEF;
        address[] memory slashingHandlers = new address[](1);
        slashingHandlers[0] = slashingHandler;
        core.allowlistAssets(assets, slashingHandlers);
        vm.prank(operator);
        IKarakBaseVault[] memory vaultsDeployed = core.deployVaults(vaultConfigs, core.implementation( address(vault)) );
        VaultLib.Config memory config = vaultsDeployed[0].vaultConfig();
// we can see that the asset of the vault is DeadBeef wich indicate the native asset but with the implementation for the ERC20 tokens.
        assertEq(config.asset, Constants.DEAD_BEEF);
        assertEq(core.implementation(address(vaultsDeployed[0])), core.implementation(address(vault)));

    }

````

This test show that an operator can deploy a vault with any implementation allowlisted and any allowlisted asset. 



